---
title: 'Exploring the LLM Pareto frontier of quality, cost and speed'
---

# Introduction

Stencila Cloud's model router provides you with the "best" model for your input.
It does this by analyzing the outputs of various benchmarking services to
produce normalized scores for quality, speed and cost.

This document describes what that means and how that's achieved.

```python exec always
# generating a list of model names
import pandas as pd

df = pd.read_parquet("../data/routing/scoring.parquet")

model_names = df["model"].unique()
model_names
```

::: table 

Large languages models' performance in cost, quality and speed

```python exec auto
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
from decimal import Decimal

df = pd.read_parquet("../data/routing/scoring.parquet")

df['cost'] = df['cost'] * Decimal('1000000.0')
df['cost'] = df['cost'].astype(np.float64).round(2)
df['quality'] = df['quality'].astype(np.float64).round(2)
df['speed'] = df['speed'].astype(np.float64).round(2)

pd.DataFrame({
    'Model': df['model'],
    'Cost ($ per million tokens)': df['cost'],
    'Quality (Elo ranking)': df['quality'],
    'Speed (tokens per second)': df['speed'],
})
```

:::

Because each metric uses such different ranges, we normalize them to percentile rankings.

::: table 

Large languages models' (normalized) performance in cost, quality and speed

```python exec auto
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
from decimal import Decimal

df = pd.read_parquet("../data/routing/scoring.parquet")

df['quality_pct'] = df['quality'].rank() / len(df['quality'].dropna())
df['cost_pct'] = df['cost'].rank() / len(df['cost'].dropna())
df['speed_pct'] = df['speed'].rank() / len(df['speed'].dropna())

pd.DataFrame({
    'Model': df['model'],
    'Cost (percentile ranking)': df['cost_pct'].round(1),
    'Quality (percentile ranking)': df['quality_pct'].round(1),
    'Speed (percentile ranking)': df['speed_pct'].round(1),
})
```

:::

Now that we have normalized data, it's possible to see how models compare against all three metrics.

One way to do this is to compare variables against a common one. For example, we generally expect 
that model performance correlates with its cost. Is that accurate?

