::: table

Statistical tests for normality of quality scores distribution.

```python exec
from scipy import stats

# Perform normality tests
shapiro_stat, shapiro_p = stats.shapiro(df['quality'])
anderson_result = stats.anderson(df['quality'], dist='norm')
ks_stat, ks_p = stats.kstest(df['quality'], 'norm', args=(df['quality'].mean(), df['quality'].std()))

# Create results dataframe
pd.DataFrame({
    'Test': ['Shapiro-Wilk', 'Anderson-Darling', 'Kolmogorov-Smirnov'],
    'Statistic': [shapiro_stat, anderson_result.statistic, ks_stat],
    'p-value': [shapiro_p, np.nan, ks_p]
}).round(3)
```

:::

::: create @code-chunk [anthropic/claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022] use statistical tests to evaluate how well the quality metric in the df dataframe matches a normal distribution >>>

```python exec
# Import scipy for statistical tests
from scipy import stats

# Calculate basic statistics for quality scores
quality_scores = df['quality']
mean = quality_scores.mean()
std = quality_scores.std()

# Run Shapiro-Wilk test for normality
shapiro_stat, shapiro_p = stats.shapiro(quality_scores)

# Run Anderson-Darling test for normality
anderson_result = stats.anderson(quality_scores, dist='norm')

# Run QQ plot
fig, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 5))

# Histogram with normal curve overlay
hist_kwargs = dict(bins=10, density=True, alpha=0.7)
ax1.hist(quality_scores, **hist_kwargs)
x = np.linspace(min(quality_scores), max(quality_scores), 100)
ax1.plot(x, stats.norm.pdf(x, mean, std), 'r-', lw=2, 
         label=f'Normal\nμ={mean:.1f}\nσ={std:.1f}')
ax1.set_title('Histogram with Normal Curve')
ax1.legend()

# QQ plot
stats.probplot(quality_scores, dist="norm", plot=ax2)
ax2.set_title('Q-Q Plot')

plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()

# Print test results
print("\nShapiro-Wilk test:")
print(f"Statistic: {shapiro_stat:.3f}")
print(f"p-value: {shapiro_p:.3f}")

print("\nAnderson-Darling test:")
print(f"Statistic: {anderson_result.statistic:.3f}")
print("Critical values:", anderson_result.critical_values)
print("Significance levels:", anderson_result.significance_level)
```

