ax[1].set_xlabel('Cost Percentile')

ax[2].hist(df['speed_pct'], bins=20, color='salmon', edgecolor='black')
ax[2].set_xlabel('Speed Percentile')

plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()
```

What happens when we look for the thing?

::: figure

A comparison of quality, cost, and speed metrics across models, showing the percentile ranks of each measure.

```python exec
plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))

plt.scatter(df['quality_pct'], df['cost_pct'], label='Cost', alpha=0.6)
plt.scatter(df['quality_pct'], df['speed_pct'], label='Speed', alpha=0.6)

plt.xlabel('Quality Percentile')
plt.ylabel('Percentile Rank')
plt.legend()
plt.grid(True, alpha=0.3)

plt.tight_layout()
plt.show()
```

:::

# Model selection

Many benchmarking systems lack sufficient information to uniquely identify a
specific model. For example, when a benchmarking report uses the key
`gpt-3.5-turbo`, it's unclear whether this refers to `gpt-3.5-turbo-0125`,
`gpt-3.5-turbo-0301`, or another variant. Typically benchmarking providers
update their reports to point to the most current variant, but this policy and
its timing is not consistent.

To avoid data corruption, we skip these records when specific identifiers are
available. This extends to skipping benchmarks from models that are named with a
`-latest` suffix. Rather than using `codestral-latest`, we use records with
`codestral-2405`.

Because reconciling between different providers is time
consuming and brittle, we skip these records and use model identifiers with
specific identifiers.

# Name normalization

Each provider uses a slightly different naming convention. We normalize these to
match the format `<model>-<version>-<size>-<task>-<discriminator>-<quantization>`.

For example:

::: table

Examples of model normalization within Stencila

| Original key                               | Normalized key           | Remarks                                                                |
| ------------------------------------------ | ------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `anthropic.claude-v2:1`                    | `claude-2`               | Provider and configuration version removed. Version number normalized. |
| `chatgpt-4o-latest-20241120`               | `gpt-4o-2024-11-20`      | Name and identifier normalized to OpenAI's naming convention           |
| `claude1`                                  | `claude-1`               | Hyphen added between model name and version                            |
| `cloudflare/@cf/meta/llama-2-7b-chat-fp16` | `llama-2.7b-chat-fp16`   | Provider removed. Version number normalized.                           |
| `command-r7b-12-2024`                      | `command-r-7b-12-2024`   | Hyphen added between model name and size                               |
| `deepseek-v2.5-1210`                       | `deepseek-2.5-1210`      | `v` stripped from version                                              |
| `deepseekr1`                               | `deepseek-r1`            | Hyphen added to name to allow lexicographic sorting                    |
| `llama-3-70b-chat-hf`                      | `llama-3-70b-chat-fp16`  | `hf` normalized to `fp16`                                              |
| `llama-v3p1-8b-instruct`                   | `llama-3.1-8b-instruct`  | `v` stripped from version, period added to version                     |
| `Mixtral-8x7B-Instruct-v0.1`               | `mixtral-8x7b-instruct`  | Case normalized. Configuration version removed                         |
| `us.meta.llama3-3-70b-instruct-v1:0`       | `llama-3.3-70b-instruct` | Provider and configuration version removed. Version number normalized. |

:::

While the primary intention of this normalization is to ensure that the model keys are consistent between providers over time,
it is also possible to decompose the normalized key to infer more information about the model at hand.

::: table

Interpreting Stencila's model keys

| Normalized key                 | Model name    | Version     | Size  | Task       | Discriminator | Quantization |
| ------------------------------ | ------------- | ----------- | ----- | ---------- | ------------- | ------------ |
| `claude-1`                     | `claude`      | `1`         |       |            |               |              |
| `command-r-7b-12-2024`         | `command-r`   |             | `7b`  |            | `12-2024`     |              |
| `deepseek-r1`                  | `deepseek-r1` |             |       |            |               |              |
| `gemma-2-9b-it`                | `gemma`       | `2`         | `9b`  | `it`       |               |              |
| `gpt-4o-2024-11-20`            | `gpt-4o`      |             |       |            | `2024-11-20`  |              |
| `llama-2-7b-chat-fp16`         | `llama`       | `2`         | `7b`  | `chat`     |               | `fp16`       |
| `llama-3-70b-chat-hf`          | `llama`       | `3`         | `70b` | `chat`     |               | `fp16`       |
| `llama-3.1-8b-instruct`        | `llama`       | `3.1`       | `8b`  | `instruct` |               |              |
| `gemini-1.5-flash-8b-exp-0827` | `gemini`      | `1.5-flash` | `8b`  |            | `exp-0827`    |              |

:::

The identifier field is typically a date, which we avoid normalizing.

Some providers indicate their model's quantization in the key. Quantization
describes how model weights are represented within the model. Conventionally,
models use 32-bit floating point numbers to represent weights. It's possible to
increase throughput and decrease memory usage by reducing the numerical
precision of these weights, typically at the expense of quality. A model is said
to be _quantized_ if that process has been carried out in a published model.
For example, `fp16` indicates that the model is quantized to half-precision
floating point numbers and `int8` indicates that the model uses 8-bit integers
for model weights.

::: table

Quantization schemes for model weights used by Stencila

| Key   | Description                                   |
| ----- | --------------------------------------------- |
| bfp16 | bfloat16 ("brain floating point")             |
| fp16  | 16-bit floating point ("IEEE half-precision") |
| fp8   | 8-bit floating point  ("8-bit minifloat")     |
| int8  | 8-bit integers                                |
| int4  | 4-bit integers                                |

:::

Models with openly-published weights, sometimes referred to as _open weights_ or
_open source_ models, have more naming diversity. They spawn derivatives,
through processes called _fine-tuning_ which  from these base models.

Model size. The size of a model is typically indicated by the number of
parameters that it has. For example, a model with 70 billion parameters will
typically include `70b` in its name. Smaller variants of models are _distilled_
from larger models.

LiteLLM might use the key `meta.llama3-8b-instruct-v1:0`, which we convert to
`llama-3-8b-instruct`. The original schema is
`<provider>.<model><model-version>-<size>-<task>-<configuration-version>`. Our
processing removes the provider and configuration version, and normalizes the
model name to lowercase and ensures that a hyphen exists between .
